al solution of the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/state.json" "file_sha256" "5832cf73f90b8c6e6c8cfdc6a77cd9d303b04436fb4aa379400381b6d6ca9e9c"} {"question" "q-20783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+4y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-4y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-4y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/state.json" "file_sha256" "5832cf73f90b8c6e6c8cfdc6a77cd9d303b04436fb4aa379400381b6d6ca9e9c"} {"question" "q-20783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bcec689ef0e7a2d099946511c921233b579bcaa37c05623c4d37f4a77f864b39"} {"question" "q-20783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/state.json" "file_sha256" "5832cf73f90b8c6e6c8cfdc6a77cd9d303b04436fb4aa379400381b6d6ca9e9c"} {"question" "q-20783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2+4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/state.json" "file_sha256" "5832cf73f90b8c6e6c8cfdc6a77cd9d303b04436fb4aa379400381b6d6ca9e9c"} {"question" "q-20783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/state.json" "file_sha256" "5832cf73f90b8c6e6c8cfdc6a77cd9d303b04436fb4aa379400381b6d6ca9e9c"} {"question" "e-4699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/example-4699.json" "file_sha256" "cb0b97387d73b996adebc98edfee72459a458c1cbc5690e11dd0cca32a2549e9"} {"question" "e-4699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3y=1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=1+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1+3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=⟨1+3y,\\,y⟩,\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/example-4699.json" "file_sha256" "cb0b97387d73b996adebc98edfee72459a458c1cbc5690e11dd0cca32a2549e9"} {"question" "e-4700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=4 \\\\ 4x+10y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/example-4700.json" "file_sha256" "a5d252a64d8aa411380c1727a7cf5c2f290874e8828e3ba001d836d3591d7350"} {"question" "e-4700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{5}{2}y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-8$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/example-4700.json" "file_sha256" "a5d252a64d8aa411380c1727a7cf5c2f290874e8828e3ba001d836d3591d7350"} {"question" "q-42167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "327e15bd24c838b7eafbeae7c5d06bee461068358b701321cbd76ca7f2a43960" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Divide the first augmented row by 7 to obtain [1,2,3], then subtract three times that row from the second to obtain [0,0,0]. Thus the system reduces to x+2y=3, giving x=3-2y with y arbitrary real. Original option 1 and the stored bmatrix answer both express exactly this solution. The saved Incorrect grade applies to the submitted -3-2y option, not this reviewed answer. This is a source-backed derived judgment from the original problem and authentic worked solution, not a successful-grade inference." "bindings" {"problem" "adda58be9c4859eba91ea61251894858afec2edddb2043ffd99ed39aacc766c5" "worked_solution" "829696a85c702070b66349555a62ddc4f30ba4a13c5bdb1725dfbcfb18280a58" "original_field" "f74554fe50f999353c9fc32f182e4c1f31f57f15009334b39f024fa180f5777c" "reviewed_field" "c3c02099bb73a7b35d50b55c2d6ac308454b900b6556e8414b118da350e91274" "raw_widget" "da75b9a3e7c44673f73081db09b6e46eb9d0a35a00d0370dc7074011cf3d6e7e"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-42167-before.png" "sha256" "ab0e92c79b70d277bda21a90c7fa0b3f604d0d0ee85cb78bc69032d70ff022c6"} {"path" "q-42167-after.json" "sha256" "96f0f7da74ceed08c369deb97b298024bfc3b1dc6fa54129a4609e24f52d0c1d"} {"path" "history-q-42167.png" "sha256" "b9ce84a3acdcc75e6424e58b1cd0628f5a0d5e3d8b5b268a15b8e8951104c37e"} {"path" "history-q-42167.json" "sha256" "b20b31fb422424729c409332a3109b15100c615849631f87c67a274d6d2b39cb"}]} {"question" "q-42167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["text" "No solution"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040382/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "327e15bd24c838b7eafbeae7c5d06bee461068358b701321cbd76ca7f2a43960" "rationale" "Divide the first augmented row by 7 to obtain [1,2,3], then subtract three times that row from the second to obtain [0,0,0]. Thus the system reduces to x+2y=3, giving x=3-2y with y arbitrary real. Original option 1 and the stored bmatrix answer both express exactly this solution. The saved Incorrect grade applies to the submitted -3-2y option, not this reviewed answer. This is a source-backed derived judgment from the original problem and authentic worked solution, not a successful-grade inference."}] "usage" {"e-4699" [] "q-42157" [] "q-42167" [] "e-4700" []} "basis" 1437 "retractions" [[17592186106245 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n${7x + 14y = 21; 3x + 6y = 9$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system."] [17592186106245 :question/answer-fields 17592186103979] [17592186106243 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n${x - 6y = 2; - 4x + 24y =-10$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system."] [17592186106243 :question/answer-fields 17592186103967] [17592186105525 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."] [17592186105525 :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1; 0x + 0y = 0\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y = 0\n$$\n\nwe can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y)$, we get\n\n$$\nx=1+3y\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\nx = 1 + 3y; y = y\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = ⟨1 + 3y, y⟩, y ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$"] [17592186105526 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n$2x + 5y = 4; 4x + 10y = 0$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system."] [17592186105526 :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx + \\frac{5}{2}y = 2; 0x + 0y =-8\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y=-8\n$$\n\nhas no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8$, which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186103968 17592186103969 17592186103970 17592186103971 17592186103972 17592186103980 17592186103981 17592186103982 17592186103983 17592186103984] "immutable_fields" [[17592186103967 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103967 :answer-field/id] [17592186103967 :answer-field/key] [17592186103967 :answer-field/type] [17592186103979 :answer-field/correct] [17592186103979 :answer-field/id] [17592186103979 :answer-field/key] [17592186103979 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
